---
// One date dimension playing two roles for the returns fact: order date and return date.
---
<figure class="rpd">
  <div class="rpd-grid">
    <div class="rpd-role">order_date view<span>dim_date, renamed: order_month, order_weekday</span></div>
    <div class="rpd-fact">
      <b>fact_returns</b>
      <code>order_date_key → 20251128</code>
      <code>return_date_key → 20251210</code>
      <code>days_to_return = 12</code>
    </div>
    <div class="rpd-role">return_date view<span>dim_date, renamed: return_month, return_weekday</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="rpd-base">both are the same table: <code>dim_date</code> (731 rows, one per day)</div>
  <figcaption>A role-playing dimension is one physical table joined more than once, each time in a different role. Give every role its own alias or view with its own column names, so a report can never mix up "ordered in November" with "returned in November".</figcaption>
</figure>
<style is:inline>
.rpd { margin: 20px 0; }
.rpd-grid { display: grid; gap: 8px; grid-template-columns: 1fr; text-align: center; }
@media (min-width: 560px) { .rpd-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.2fr 1fr; align-items: center; } }
.rpd-role { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 8px 10px; background: var(--accent-soft); font-family: ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 14px; }
.rpd-role span { display: block; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 2px; }
.rpd-fact { border: 2px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 10px; padding: 8px 10px; background: var(--surface); display: grid; gap: 3px; font-size: 13px; }
.rpd-fact code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.rpd-base { margin-top: 8px; text-align: center; border: 1px dashed var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 6px 10px; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--muted); }
.rpd figcaption { margin-top: 8px; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--muted); }
</style>
